WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.920
Writing is powerful.

00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:06.480
Hi there Steve Kaufmann here, and
today I want to talk about the sixth

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:12.690
of my, the sixth, number six of my,
uh, personal priority in language

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:15.210
learning goals and that is writing.

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:19.350
Remember, if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:20.850
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:25.680
And if you follow me on a podcast
service, which I recommend, uh, if

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:29.160
you're working on improving your language
skills, your English language skills,

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:32.220
uh, please leave a review there.

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:33.180
All right.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:38.820
So, remember that these goals are,
this is my own priority of goals.

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:43.349
So this is not necessarily what I
would recommend as a priority of

00:00:43.349 --> 00:00:45.449
goals for everyone in every situation.

00:00:45.449 --> 00:00:46.920
But it's my situation.

00:00:46.949 --> 00:00:47.280
All right.

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:53.889
For me, writing comes at the end of
my, or near the end of my um, language

00:00:53.889 --> 00:00:58.510
learning goals, even though all of these
goals are somewhat interchangeable.

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:03.339
But remember that I say my first goal
is to increase my vocabulary, acquire

00:01:03.339 --> 00:01:08.380
words, largely passive vocabulary
that enables me then to start reading.

00:01:09.005 --> 00:01:12.635
And once I read, then I can be in
to understand what I'm listening to.

00:01:12.635 --> 00:01:17.075
So then listening comes after reading
as number three, and then once I've

00:01:17.075 --> 00:01:20.405
been doing a lot of listening, I
start to feel I can start saying

00:01:20.405 --> 00:01:22.415
something to start speaking.

00:01:23.195 --> 00:01:26.495
And when I started speaking, then I
realize that I need to improve the

00:01:26.555 --> 00:01:29.705
accuracy of my use of the language.

00:01:29.735 --> 00:01:32.405
In other words, to focus
in a little bit on grammar.

00:01:32.555 --> 00:01:33.905
So that's number five.

00:01:35.325 --> 00:01:37.830
Now...therefore then comes writing.

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:43.950
Now writing, I personally don't do a
lot of writing now in the languages

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:47.550
that I'm learning, but some of the
languages that I speak quite well,

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:52.500
like French and Chinese, when I learned
those languages, I did a lot of writing.

00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:56.545
And there's no question that
writing is a powerful way to

00:01:56.545 --> 00:01:58.855
improve our language skills.

00:01:59.425 --> 00:02:00.985
Uh, and it has a number...

00:02:01.045 --> 00:02:05.305
it's kind of, it's a form of output, just
like speaking is a form of output, but

00:02:05.305 --> 00:02:10.255
it has a big advantage over speaking and
that is that when you write, you have

00:02:10.255 --> 00:02:16.455
the chance to look things up uh, check
the conjugation table or the declension

00:02:16.455 --> 00:02:21.375
tables, or look up a word, uh, or maybe
look at some of the phrases that you

00:02:21.385 --> 00:02:23.265
saved on LingQ and try to use them.

00:02:23.505 --> 00:02:27.885
In other words, you aren't forced,
as in speaking, to just use

00:02:27.885 --> 00:02:31.305
whatever comes to mind, you can
actually do a little more research.

00:02:31.455 --> 00:02:36.645
You can actually improve the accuracy
of your output and your ability to

00:02:36.645 --> 00:02:41.625
express yourself in the more sort
of relaxed atmosphere of writing.

00:02:41.774 --> 00:02:44.595
So it's definitely
something that I recommend.

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:51.780
Now, one of the difficulties is,
so when I was learning Chinese, I

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:53.700
was assigned writing assignments.

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:54.480
I had to write.

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:58.860
Uh, in French I was a, first of
all, I had French at school and then

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:00.780
in France, I had essays to write.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:01.680
I had things to write.

00:03:02.270 --> 00:03:07.370
So you have to find a meaningful
things to write about, um, because

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:08.960
you don't want to just write stuff.

00:03:08.990 --> 00:03:10.190
So, so what can you do?

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:12.920
Well you can keep a diary,
if you're that way inclined.

00:03:13.490 --> 00:03:18.080
Uh, you can, uh, you know, there's
so many opportunities to, through

00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:26.265
email or uh, different, uh, chat
rooms or, uh, maintaining a blog.

00:03:26.295 --> 00:03:30.135
I mean, the, the sky's the limit, uh,
communicating with people or just keeping

00:03:30.135 --> 00:03:34.125
track of your own activities or what
you're doing in the target language.

00:03:34.125 --> 00:03:39.015
There are many opportunities,
probably more than, uh, when I was

00:03:39.015 --> 00:03:43.665
learning 50 years ago of having
meaningful occasions where you

00:03:43.665 --> 00:03:46.825
can write and writing is powerful.

00:03:46.825 --> 00:03:50.924
You will improve your grasp of
vocabulary, you will improve

00:03:50.924 --> 00:03:53.924
your grasp of grammar by writing.

00:03:54.225 --> 00:03:59.295
Now, obviously there are difficulties
if you're learning a language with a

00:03:59.295 --> 00:04:05.114
different writing system, um, which
has been the case for me, and certainly

00:04:05.114 --> 00:04:09.165
in Greek and Ukrainian and Russian and
Persian and Arabic, but there again,

00:04:09.495 --> 00:04:15.105
because we now have the ability to
type it's, it's a lot easier to type

00:04:15.105 --> 00:04:19.605
the language uh, to write online and
as you're writing online to look up

00:04:19.605 --> 00:04:23.625
words and look up grammar points,
it's more difficult to write by hand.

00:04:24.015 --> 00:04:30.474
Uh, writing by hand is something that
I have not done a lot of, but I, I

00:04:30.495 --> 00:04:35.775
think there's some evidence that when
you write by hand, you improve your

00:04:35.775 --> 00:04:37.755
ability to retain what you're learning.

00:04:38.505 --> 00:04:43.844
Again, because I am a dilettante
learner I'm not under any

00:04:43.844 --> 00:04:45.914
obligation to learn anything.

00:04:45.914 --> 00:04:50.115
So I do whatever is easiest
and writing online is easier.

00:04:50.325 --> 00:04:52.635
I can go even further.

00:04:53.025 --> 00:04:55.485
Uh, I can dictate on my iPad.

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:00.550
And so I get away from the idea of
writing whatsoever, but I still have

00:05:00.550 --> 00:05:04.600
some of the advantages of writing
because I see what I dictated.

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:11.350
I can go in and correct it, or change
some words or, or, uh, somehow, um,

00:05:11.380 --> 00:05:15.479
you know, still have that advantage of
writing and that is that you have more

00:05:15.479 --> 00:05:19.979
time to express yourself, but I don't
have to remember how to form the letters.

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:24.270
Uh, so these are different ways that
you can engage in writing as a means

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:27.419
of developing your output, uh, ability.

00:05:27.479 --> 00:05:32.310
The other thing is when we write
some people maybe think that

00:05:32.310 --> 00:05:37.200
they have to write, uh, in a
more formal way than they speak.

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:40.380
And perhaps in some situations you do.

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:43.500
There's formal communications,
business letters and so forth,

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:45.300
less and less the case today.

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:50.250
My approach to writing has always
been to write the way I speak.

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:56.730
So I tend to, you know, develop a
somewhat neutral way of speaking.

00:05:56.730 --> 00:05:59.520
So I'm not too informal,
not too much slang.

00:05:59.910 --> 00:06:03.090
And then my writing is just
an extension of my speaking.

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:04.650
So I write the way I speak.

00:06:05.010 --> 00:06:12.295
So if you have a neutral, fairly,
not formal, but sort of standard

00:06:12.295 --> 00:06:16.735
way of expressing yourself when you
speak, then that kind of becomes the

00:06:16.735 --> 00:06:18.925
way you express yourself in writing.

00:06:19.315 --> 00:06:22.315
And I personally find that it's
easier to do that, to make your

00:06:22.315 --> 00:06:24.085
writing an extension of your speaking.

00:06:24.925 --> 00:06:29.515
Also, when it comes to writing people
say, well, you know, there's business

00:06:29.515 --> 00:06:33.025
English and there's medical English, and
there's this English or other languages.

00:06:33.055 --> 00:06:35.655
There's a sort of special English.

00:06:36.245 --> 00:06:40.740
Here again, you're only going to be
able to write as well as you read.

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:46.560
So if you need a special academic English
or French or whatever the language

00:06:46.590 --> 00:06:53.400
may be, or business English or medical
English, you have to consume a lot

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:56.340
of content, you know, in that domain.

00:06:57.510 --> 00:07:01.080
Uh, if you want to write in a
more literary way, then you have

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:03.240
to consume a lot of literature.

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:05.130
The best way...

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:10.560
the best thing you can do to write
better is to read more because that

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:16.890
way, sort of a correct expression, a
somewhat more sophisticated expression.

00:07:17.330 --> 00:07:24.510
More elegant, more eloquent more, accurate
expression will become second nature

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:28.620
because you've done so much listening
and reading of that kind of material.

00:07:29.070 --> 00:07:32.700
Of course you can have your writing
corrected, but my experience is

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:37.380
that corrections aren't as effective
as a means of improving in the

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:41.070
language as more and more exposure.

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:45.239
So people who want to improve
their ability to write still have

00:07:45.239 --> 00:07:49.140
to focus in on the previous goals.

00:07:49.140 --> 00:07:51.270
In other words, acquiring
a large vocabulary.

00:07:52.095 --> 00:07:56.025
Uh, do a lot of reading,
do a lot of listening.

00:07:56.445 --> 00:07:59.955
Even your speaking activity is going
to improve your ability to write.

00:08:00.405 --> 00:08:03.855
And of course, to the extent that you
spend time on grammar, it's also going

00:08:03.855 --> 00:08:05.295
to improve your ability to write.

00:08:05.535 --> 00:08:10.605
So you have to do all of these things
in order to become better at writing.

00:08:10.785 --> 00:08:13.655
Don't rely on having
your writing corrected.

00:08:13.685 --> 00:08:18.935
It's probably the least, uh,
important activity in terms of

00:08:18.935 --> 00:08:20.525
improving your ability to write.

00:08:20.855 --> 00:08:25.265
So there are my thoughts on
writing my fifth, sixth goal.

00:08:25.594 --> 00:08:29.735
Uh, I did a couple of videos on
the subject of writing a while

00:08:29.735 --> 00:08:34.044
back, one is called Write More to
Improve Your Language Accuracy.

00:08:34.284 --> 00:08:37.020
The other one is called The Importance
of Writing in Language Learning.

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:41.430
You might want to have a look at those to
see some more discussion of this subject.

00:08:41.850 --> 00:08:43.440
So thank you for listening.

00:08:43.470 --> 00:08:43.990
Bye for now.

